(project tuna-trace
 (* agent note (|2026-09-17, F1 follow-through.  The firing-repeat pilot
    measured the crown's redundancy out-of-band: a probe with the v1
    gate replaced by a counter (scratch-firing/, untracked).  This
    chapter makes per-run firing evidence a first-class stored fact:
    an opt-in event log over the counted firings, replay-adjacent but
    never part of a run's verdict.  Push stays roerick's call.|) *)
 (doc "Tuna book chapter: trace - the firing-event collector: opt-in, capped observability over the counted firings. A trace is a reading of a run, not a second opinion about it: the counting law is untouched.")
 (status implemented)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (section observation-only
  (doc "Traced and untraced runs agree on every number")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        The collector observes; it never influences.  An event is noted
        AFTER the counting law has spoken, so recording touches no
        fuel, no steps, no evaluation order, no clock poll.  A traced
        run and an untraced run of the same program+inputs produce the
        same normal form and the same step count - a tested invariant
        (trace suite: form equality + step equality under both laws),
        not a hope.

        This is the rule-4 boundary again, pointed at reading: the
        count stays canonical under the law that produced the run
        row; the trace just shows the firings behind the number.
       |))))
 (section event-kinds
  (doc "fire / charge / dirty / loop; hits are counters, never events")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        v0: every counted firing emits a [fire] event - one event per
        step, recorded = steps.  Events carry the triage arm that
        fired (fork(stem,_), fork(fork,_)/leaf, wrapper, ...), so a
        v0 trace reads as the rule sequence, not just a count.

        v1: [charge] events are the memo table filling - one per
        counted step.  Memo HITS are aggregated counters (tr_hits),
        never events: the crown family fires 2M times for ~26k
        distinct pairs, and the event log would be the 2M-row story.
        The counters tell it instead: raw = charged + hits (+1 when a
        loop fired).

        [dirty] marks a firing that answered a prim inside and
        therefore re-executes (sharing.borg's prim-exemption law made
        visible per firing: grant re-checks and journal rows fire on
        every occurrence, and the trace shows each one).

        [loop] closes a divergent run: in-flight re-entry, note
        "in-flight re-entry", carrying the offending (fun, arg)
        pair.  The run answers Loop at omega = 3 steps; the event is
        the last one recorded.
       |))))
 (section digests
  (doc "16-hex prefixes of the memo law's content digests")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Event fun/arg fields carry 16-hex prefixes of the SAME
        structural content digests the v1 memo keys use (sha256 over
        the canonical ternary form), so a trace reads against
        tree_values and replay.

        Cost: v1 events reuse the digests the memo law already
        computed for its keys - free.  Pure-v0 traces digest on
        demand, and only for small trees, bounding display cost on
        the canonical side.  Both share one per-trace cache keyed on
        physical node identity (persistent trees share subterm
        objects exactly when the sharing is real); a cache miss
        hashes the node once.  Correctness never depends on the
        physical fast path - physically-distinct-but-equal subtrees
        just hash twice.
       |))))
 (section caps-and-counters
  (doc "The cap bounds the RECORDING, never the accounting")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        tr_cap bounds RECORDED events only.  The counters (raw
        firings, hits, dirty re-executions, loop flag) count the
        whole run regardless; when the cap bites, the truncated flag
        says so and the head of the story is what got kept (seq
        stays dense and ordered - pages replay from seq 0).

        Server ceiling: TUNA_TRACE_MAX_EVENTS (default 50,000).  A
        request asks for less, never more: trace:true takes the
        server cap, trace_cap:N is clamped to it.  Absent, false,
        or a non-positive trace_cap records no trace - absence is
        the safe default, not an error.
       |))))
 (section surface
  (doc "API + run page: read-only traces on the run surface")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        POST /api/runs takes optional trace / trace_cap (JSON, next
        to semantics).  GET /api/runs/:id/trace?after=&limit= returns
        the summary + seq-ordered event pages (limit default 200,
        max 1000); 404 when the run has no trace - an untraced run
        and an unknown id are indistinguishable on purpose, there is
        no trace-shaped lie to tell about either.

        The run page grows a trace row ("21 events (raw 26 · charged
        21 · hits 5)", muted "untraced" otherwise) and /runs/:id/trace
        renders the summary table + event rows with plain next-page
        links, no JS.  Session auth as usual.

        The REPL surface takes no trace field.  Traces are a
        run-surface feature; a traced REPL round is one POST /api/runs
        away.  Replay and the verifier ignore traces entirely: the
        run row's numbers and the journal chain remain the
        authoritative verdict; a missing or tampered trace cannot
        change either.
       |))))
 (section storage
  (doc "0010: summary row + event rows, events first")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Migration 0010: run_traces (PK run_id, ON DELETE CASCADE:
        traces die with their run) and trace_events (PK (run_id,
        seq)).  The summary carries the counters and flags; events
        carry kind, rule, both digest prefixes, note.

        Insert order is the integrity rule: events FIRST, summary
        row LAST - a partial insert never advertises a trace whose
        head events are missing (fetch is keyed on the summary).
        Events insert in chunks of 400 rows per statement, under the
        65535-parameter ceiling.  Bulk data, GC-able under the
        journals' retention policy when one exists.
       |))))
 (section f1-instrument
  (doc "What the trace is for: eyes on the engine split")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        The F1 pilot measured repeat-rate from the v0 side with an
        out-of-band counter; the measurement died with the probe
        process.  The trace makes the same evidence a stored,
        queryable fact per run: how many distinct firings the memo
        law charged, where hits short-circuited subtrees, which
        firings re-executed under the dirty rule, whether the run
        re-entered itself.

        The engine call itself stays where it has been: demand-memo
        port vs v1-loop is roerick's decision, and this chapter
        takes no side.  It only insists that whichever engine runs,
        its firings can be read afterwards - the crown's 25,813
        charged steps and the plateau behind them should be a row
        you can SELECT, not a number in a conversation.
       |)))))
